<!--Post: 10804--><!--Time: 1110232896-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10804"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Skov<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Skov<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, March 07, 2005 - 2:01 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Here is some more to think about concerning Melissa Scott as a possible BTK victim. The thing to look at is the adress,409 E. Main, wich in a common factor in all this insidents: <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.arkvalleynews.com/web/isite.dll?1107462969820" target=_top>http://www.arkvalleynews.com/web/isite.dll?1107462 969820</a> <BR> <BR>Woman, 27, found dead at home in VC <BR> <BR>A Valley Center woman was found dead Monday morning in her home at 409 E. Main. <BR> <BR>Valley Center police responded to a 911 call with information that a boy had found his mother unconscious. Police reached the home and attempted to treat Melissa Scott, 27, with CPR before EMS personnel arrived. <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.arkvalleynews.com/web/isite.dll?1079557208250~409" target=_top>http://www.arkvalleynews.com/web/isite.dll?1079557 208250~409</a> <BR> <BR>March 18, 2004 <BR>March 9--2:27 p.m. Compliance Officer Cindy Plant &#40;skovs note: she trained Dennis Rader for Sedgwick County Animal Control and had asked for him to be placed on the board.&#41;  impounded a stray silver and gray male husky at 409 E. Main. <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.arkvalleynews.com/web/isite.dll?1025198167551~409" target=_top>http://www.arkvalleynews.com/web/isite.dll?1025198 167551~409</a> <BR> <BR>June 27, 2002 <BR>June 21--9:43 p.m. Officer Ken Harper assisted EMS with a person who was choking at 409 E. Main. The person was OK, the officer reported. <BR> <BR> <BR>This was also posted ower at AMW, go read the thred: <BR><a href="http://forums.prospero.com/foxamw/messages?msg=2540.40" target=_top>http://forums.prospero.com/foxamw/messages?msg=254 0.40</a> <BR> <BR>From:  my2sence   Feb-28 10:39 am   <BR>To:  skovdk   &#40;1 of 11&#41;   <BR>  <BR>  2455.1   <BR>  <BR>At the time of your posting on the death of Ms. Scott, I thought you were on to something.  It seemed strange that there wasn&#39;t much infomation. Now knowing that DR lived and worked in the same area as Scott, I am even more curious. I did find this. Where I live, the dog police work in pairs. Do you know if Ms. Scott would have lived at 409 E. Main at the time of the dog report? Any other info about Ms. Scott? <BR> <BR>A Valley Center woman was found dead Monday morning in her home at 409 E. Main. <BR> <BR>Valley Center police responded to a 911 call with information that a boy had found his mother unconscious. Police reached the home and attempted to treat Melissa Scott, 27, with CPR before EMS personnel arrived. <BR> <BR> <BR> <BR>March 9--2:27 p.m. Compliance Officer Cindy Plant impounded a stray silver and gray male husky at 409 E. Main. <BR> <BR> <BR>     Maybe DR met Ms. Scott at that time? <BR> <BR> From:  my2sence   Feb-28 11:44 am   <BR>To:  phobicone   &#40;5 of 11&#41;   <BR>  <BR>  2455.5 in reply to 2455.3   <BR>  <BR>I thought maybe they swapped some of their &#34;on the job&#34; stories.  She sure knew him. I&#39;ve been to both towns and Valley Center and Park City are only 15 minutes away. The Ark Valley News reports for both towns. DR was such a &#34;busy body&#34; I can see him checking out where his counter part had been.   <BR> <BR>Rader was appointed by the Sedgwick County Commission to the Sedgwick County Animal Control Advisory Board in 1996. <BR> <BR>Cindy Plant, a compliance officer in the Wichita suburb of Valley Center, helped train Rader for Sedgwick County Animal Control and had asked for him to be placed on the board. <BR> <BR>Plant said Rader was “very meticulous and neat. … With him, everything was just perfect: his office, his truck, everything. … <BR> <BR>“He carried a nice leather day planner with the pens lined up perfectly in it.” <BR>  <BR> <BR>  <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
